WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.950
- [Instructor] Let's say
that we have the function

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:03.850
capital F of x,

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:06.270
which we're going to define
as the definite integral

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.760
from one to sine of x,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.830
so that's an interesting
upper bound right over there,

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.930
of two t minus one,

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.650
and of course, dt,

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.770
and what we are curious about

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:23.710
is trying to figure out
what is F prime of x going

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:24.543
to be equal to?

00:00:24.543 --> 00:00:27.230
So pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.320
All right.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:31.310
So some of you might have
been a little bit challenged

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.690
by this notion of hey,

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:34.350
instead of an x on this upper bound,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.740
I now have a sine of x.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.770
If it was just an x,

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.160
I could have used the
fundamental theorem of calculus.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.240
Just to review that,

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.920
if I had a function,
let me call it h of x,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.710
if I have h of x that was
defined as the definite integral

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.710
from one to x of two t minus one dt,

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.200
we know from the fundamental
theorem of calculus

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.170
that h prime of x

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.390
would be simply this inner function

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.160
with the t replaced by the x.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.720
It would just be two x minus
one, pretty straightforward.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.680
But this one isn't quite
as straightforward.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.150
Instead of having an x up here,

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.840
our upper bound is a sine of x.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.800
So one way to think about it
is if we were to define g of x

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.260
as being equal to sine of x,

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.710
equal to sine of x,

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:25.710
our capital F of x can be
expressed as capital F of x

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.970
is the same thing as h of,

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.580
h of, instead of an x,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:32.680
everywhere we see an x,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.530
we're replacing it with a sine of x,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:38.740
so it's h of g of x, g of x.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.490
You can see the g of x right over there.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.770
So you replace x with g of x

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.700
for where, in this expression,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.990
you get h of g of x

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.920
and that is capital F of x.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.030
Now why am I doing all of that?

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.360
Well, this might start making you think

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.850
about the chain rule.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.200
Because if this is true,

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.970
then that means that capital F prime of x

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:05.970
is going to be equal to h prime of g of x,

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.010
h prime of g of x

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.300
times g prime of x.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.910
And so what would that be?

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.500
Well, we already know
what h prime of x is,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.570
so I'll need to do this in another color.

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.930
This part right over
here is going to be equal

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.210
to everywhere we see an x here,

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:26.630
we'll replace with a g of x,

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.510
so it's going to be two,

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:32.440
two times sine of x.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.140
Two sine of x,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.720
and then minus one, minus one.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.650
This is this right over here,

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.120
and then what's g prime of x?

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.210
G prime of x,

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.360
well g prime of x is just, of course,

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.490
the derivative of sine
of x is cosine of x,

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.400
is cosine of x.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.040
So this part right over here

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.350
is going to be cosine of x.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.580
And we could keep going.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.970
We could try to,

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.140
we could try to simplify this a little bit

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.530
or rewrite it in different ways,

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:06.860
but there you have it.

